-- Singapore Penal Code 1871 — criminal breach of trust.
--
--   s 405  defines "criminal breach of trust"        (the elements)
--   s 406  punishes whoever commits it               (the charge)
--
-- A charge is laid under the PUNISHING section, so this module exports one
-- charge (s 406) over one facts record, and one ladder for the s 405 elements.
--
-- This is the row's NEGATIVE case. The reported charge it is pinned to was held
-- FUNDAMENTALLY FLAWED, and the module exists to show that the flaw is one a
-- careful encoding cannot write down at all:
--
--   Viswanathan Ramachandran v Public Prosecutor [2003] SGHC 183, in which Yong
--   Pung How CJ quoted the charge from his own earlier decision in Carl Elias
--   Moses v Public Prosecutor [1995] 3 SLR 748 and held that a charge alleging
--   entrustment with one thing and misappropriation of another states no
--   offence under s 405. The quoted charge and the amendment the court allowed
--   are both reproduced in the directives below, modulo the citation form
--   ("Penal Code (Cap 224, 1985 Rev Ed)" then, "Penal Code 1871" now) and the
--   CPC s 124 header, which this row normalises across every charge it frames.
--
-- An ISOMORPHIC encoding in INERT STYLE — see culpable-homicide-301.l4 for the
-- conventions and cheating-415-417-420.l4 for the shape. Verbatim statutory
-- prose rides as inert strings; only the operative atoms are lifted into named
-- leaves; the inert strings are mortar, never shadow copies of the leaf beside
-- them.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The text
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- 405. Whoever, being in any manner entrusted with property, or with any
--      dominion over property, dishonestly misappropriates or converts to his
--      own use that property, or dishonestly uses or disposes of that property
--      in violation of any direction of law prescribing the mode in which such
--      trust is to be discharged, or of any legal contract, express or implied,
--      which he has made touching the discharge of such trust, or intentionally
--      suffers any other person to do so, commits “criminal breach of trust”.
--      Illustrations
--      (a) A, being executor to the will of a deceased person, dishonestly
--          disobeys the law which directs him to divide the effects according
--          to the will, and appropriates them to his own use. A has committed
--          criminal breach of trust.
--      (b) A is a warehouse owner or warehouse operator. Z, going on a journey,
--          entrusts his furniture to A, under a contract that it shall be
--          returned on payment of a stipulated sum for warehouse room. A
--          dishonestly sells the goods. A has committed breach of trust.
--                                            [Act 23 of 2021 wef 01/03/2022]
--      (c) A, residing in Singapore, is agent for Z, residing in Penang. There
--          is an express or implied contract between A and Z that all sums
--          remitted by Z to A shall be invested by A according to Z's
--          direction. Z remits $5,000 to A, with directions to A to invest the
--          same in Government securities. A dishonestly disobeys the direction,
--          and employs the money in his own business. A has committed criminal
--          breach of trust.
--      (d) But if A, in the last illustration, not dishonestly, but in good
--          faith, believing that it will be more for Z's advantage to hold
--          shares in the Bank X, disobeys Z's directions, and buys shares in
--          the Bank X for Z, instead of buying Government securities, here,
--          though Z should suffer loss and should be entitled to bring a civil
--          action against A on account of that loss, yet A, not having acted
--          dishonestly, has not committed criminal breach of trust.
--      (e) A, a collector of Government money, or a clerk in a Government
--          office, is entrusted with public money, and is either directed by
--          law, or bound by a contract, express or implied, with the
--          Government, to pay into a certain treasury all the public money
--          which he holds. A dishonestly appropriates the money. A has
--          committed criminal breach of trust.
--      (f) A, a carrier, is entrusted by Z with property to be carried by land
--          or by water. A dishonestly misappropriates the property. A has
--          committed criminal breach of trust.
--                                                                   [15/2019]
--
-- 406. Whoever commits criminal breach of trust shall be punished with
--      imprisonment for a term which may extend to 7 years, or with fine, or
--      with both.
--
-- One drafting note on vintage. The 2003 judgment quotes s 405 as it then stood,
-- ending "or WILFULLY suffers any other person to do so". The in-force text
-- above reads "or INTENTIONALLY suffers", substituted by Act 15 of 2019. The
-- leaves below follow the in-force text.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The shape of the section
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- s 405 is one entrustment and one of three ways of betraying it:
--
--   ENTRUSTMENT   being in any manner entrusted with property, or with any
--                 dominion over property
--
--   FIRST LIMB    dishonestly misappropriates or converts to his own use that
--                 property
--   SECOND LIMB   dishonestly uses or disposes of that property in violation
--                 of a direction of law, or of a legal contract express or
--                 implied, touching the discharge of the trust
--   THIRD LIMB    intentionally suffers any other person to do so
--
-- "being in any manner" widens the entrustment rather than adding an element,
-- so it rides inert. The mental element differs by limb and the encoding keeps
-- that visible: the first two limbs are governed by "dishonestly" (s 24), the
-- third by "intentionally" — so a person who merely stands by while another
-- betrays the trust is outside the section unless he did so on purpose.
--
-- The particulars the charge recites but the section does not test (who
-- entrusted the property, what the property was, what the entrustment was for)
-- are STRING fields on the same record, so one record is both the ladder's
-- input and the charge's.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Why the flaw is unexpressible
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- Written for a lawyer rather than for a programmer.
--
-- The charge laid against Carl Elias Moses said two things about property. It
-- said he had been entrusted with 2,000 Overseas Union Bank warrants. It said
-- he dishonestly misappropriated the proceeds of their sale. Both of those are
-- "property" in the wide sense s 22 gives the word, and the Prosecution treated
-- that as enough. The Chief Justice did not. s 405 does not say
-- "misappropriates property"; it says "misappropriates THAT property", and
-- "that property" can only be the property the accused was entrusted with. A
-- charge that names one thing in its entrustment clause and a different thing
-- in its misappropriation clause therefore discloses no offence at all, however
-- dishonest the accused may have been. The conviction fell, and only an
-- amendment granted on appeal saved the case.
--
-- That defect is not a misjudgment. It is a defect of FORM — and a form is
-- exactly the kind of thing a language can refuse to provide. This encoding has
-- ONE place to record what the accused was entrusted with: the `property`
-- field. The entrustment clause of the charge reads that field. The
-- misappropriation clause reads THE SAME FIELD. There is no second field for
-- the second clause to disagree with, so the two halves of the recital cannot
-- name two different things — not because something checks them against each
-- other, but because there is nowhere to write the second thing down.
--
-- What the investigating officer is asked instead is the question s 405 actually
-- poses. The leaf is `misappropriates that property`, and its @desc spells it
-- out: did the accused misappropriate THE PROPERTY HE WAS ENTRUSTED WITH? On
-- these facts the honest answer is no — he delivered the warrants to DBS
-- Securities exactly as he had been directed to. So the ladder answers FALSE,
-- the builder declines to write any charge, and the refusal names the element
-- that failed. The flaw surfaces before anyone is arraigned.
--
-- The bench below walks three states of the same file, in order:
--
--   `Carl Elias Moses, as charged`
--       refused. No text is written at all, and the refusal names the limb.
--
--   `Carl Elias Moses, with the warrants misappropriated`
--       frames — but its body now reads "did dishonestly misappropriate the
--       said warrants". That is a DIFFERENT allegation, and a false one. It is
--       not the charge that was laid, and nothing in this file can produce the
--       charge that was laid.
--
--   `Carl Elias Moses, the charge amended`
--       frames the charge the court said should have been laid: entrustment
--       with the PROCEEDS, misappropriation of the proceeds. Compare the
--       amendment the court actually substituted in Viswanathan Ramachandran
--       itself — "being entrusted by WC Heraeus GmbH with the proceeds from the
--       sale of a sputtering machine amounting to US$35,000 … by dishonestly
--       misappropriating the proceeds" — which moves the entrustment onto the
--       proceeds in precisely the same way.
--
-- The middle one is the demonstration. Asked for the flawed charge, the
-- encoding does not produce it; it produces the nearest charge it CAN state,
-- and that charge alleges something else.
--
-- THE LIMIT, stated here because a reader will find it anyway.
-- `property, in short` exists only so that the recital can write "the said
-- proceeds" instead of repeating thirty words, which is what real charges do.
-- It is a short form of `property` and its @desc says so; left blank, the
-- builder repeats `property` in full, which is always correct. An officer who
-- types something unrelated into it can still get a wrong word onto the page.
-- What the encoding forecloses is the STRUCTURAL flaw — two clauses of one
-- charge resting on two different properties — because the ladder holds one
-- property and asks one question about it.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `penal-code-general`

§ `Penal Code 1871 — criminal breach of trust`

§§ `The facts of one entrustment`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 405 — the operative atoms, and the particulars CPC s 124 requires
DECLARE `CBT Facts` HAS
    particulars           IS A Particulars @desc Who is charged, and when and where the offence was committed
    `entrusted by`        IS A STRING  @desc The person or body that entrusted the property to the accused, as it is to read after "being entrusted by" — for example "Trans-Pacific Credit Pte Ltd"; leave blank if the charge does not name them
    property              IS A STRING  @desc THE ONE PROPERTY IN THIS CHARGE. What the accused was entrusted with, as it is to read after "namely," — for example "2,000 Overseas Union Bank Warrant 1994". Every later clause of the charge speaks of this same thing, because section 405 speaks of "that property".
    `property, in short`  IS A STRING  @desc A short back-reference to the SAME property, as it is to read after "the said" — for example "warrants", or "proceeds" where the property entrusted was the proceeds of a sale. Leave blank to repeat the property in full.
    `purpose of the entrustment`
                          IS A STRING  @desc What the accused was entrusted with the property for, as it is to read after the property — for example "for the purpose of delivering these warrants on behalf of the company to DBS Securities Pte Ltd"

    -- "being in any manner entrusted with property, or with any dominion over property"
    `entrusted with property`
                          IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the accused entrusted with the property named above, in any manner? "Property" means money and all other property, movable or immovable, including things in action and virtual currency (s 22).
    `entrusted with dominion over property`
                          IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the accused entrusted not with the property itself but with dominion over it — a power to deal with it belonging to someone else?

    -- "dishonestly" — s 24
    dishonestly           IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused act dishonestly — intending a wrongful gain or a wrongful loss, or in a way reasonable and honest people would call dishonest, knowing that (s 24)?

    -- first limb: "dishonestly misappropriates or converts to his own use that property"
    `misappropriates that property`
                          IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused misappropriate THE PROPERTY NAMED ABOVE — the very property entrusted, not its proceeds and not anything bought with it? Section 405 says "that property", and a charge that answers about anything else discloses no offence (Carl Elias Moses v PP; Viswanathan Ramachandran v PP).
    `converts that property to his own use`
                          IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused convert THE PROPERTY NAMED ABOVE to his own use?

    -- second limb: "dishonestly uses or disposes of that property in violation of …"
    `uses that property`  IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused use THE PROPERTY NAMED ABOVE?
    `disposes of that property`
                          IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused dispose of THE PROPERTY NAMED ABOVE?
    `any direction of law prescribing the mode in which such trust is to be discharged`
                          IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was that use or disposal in violation of a direction of law prescribing how the trust was to be discharged? (Illustration (a): an executor who disobeys the law directing him to divide the estate according to the will.)
    `any legal contract, express or implied, which he has made touching the discharge of such trust`
                          IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was that use or disposal in violation of a legal contract, express or implied, that the accused had made about how the trust was to be discharged? (Illustration (c): an agent who invests a remittance in his own business instead of as directed.)

    -- third limb: "or intentionally suffers any other person to do so"
    `intentionally suffers any other person to do so`
                          IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused deliberately allow someone else to do — DISHONESTLY, and with THE PROPERTY NAMED ABOVE — any of the things the first two limbs describe: to misappropriate it or convert it to his own use, or to use or dispose of it IN VIOLATION OF a direction of law prescribing how the trust was to be discharged, or of a legal contract, express or implied, touching the discharge of the trust? The section's "so" carries all of that, so answering TRUE asserts the other person's dishonesty as well, and, where what he did was a use or a disposal, the violation too. Mere carelessness is not enough — the section says "intentionally".

§§ `s 405 — criminal breach of trust`

@export Whether the accused "commits criminal breach of trust" within section 405 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN f IS A `CBT Facts` @desc The entrustment, and what became of the property entrusted
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `commits criminal breach of trust` f IF
        "Whoever, being in any manner"
   ...  (    f's `entrusted with property`
        OR   f's `entrusted with dominion over property` )
   ...  (        (    f's dishonestly
                 ...  (    f's `misappropriates that property`
                      OR   f's `converts that property to his own use` ) )
            ..  (    f's dishonestly
                 ...  (    f's `uses that property`
                      OR   f's `disposes of that property` )
                 ...  "in violation of"
                 ...  (    f's `any direction of law prescribing the mode in which such trust is to be discharged`
                      OR   f's `any legal contract, express or implied, which he has made touching the discharge of such trust` ) )
            OR  f's `intentionally suffers any other person to do so` )

§§ `s 406 — punishment of criminal breach of trust`

@export Whether the accused has committed an offence punishable under section 406 of the Penal Code 1871 (criminal breach of trust)
GIVEN f IS A `CBT Facts` @desc The entrustment, and what became of the property entrusted
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `offence under s 406` f IF
        "Whoever"
   ...  `commits criminal breach of trust` f

§§ `The charge`

-- s 125 requires the manner for criminal breach of trust: the accused cannot
-- answer a charge that does not say what he was entrusted with, what for, and
-- what he did with it. Read off the leaves that are TRUE, in the order the
-- section states them. Carl Elias Moses, as the charge was laid:
--
--   being entrusted with property, namely, 2,000 Overseas Union Bank Warrant
--   1994 for the purpose of delivering these warrants on behalf of
--   Trans-Pacific Credit Pte Ltd (“the company”) to DBS Securities Pte Ltd
--   pursuant to a sale (per contract number 414270/501) of the said warrants,
--   and thereafter to pay the proceeds of the said sale to the company, did
--   dishonestly misappropriate the said proceeds
--
-- Note where the builder differs from that, and why: the last three words come
-- from `property, in short`, which is a short form of `property`. The charge as
-- laid could say "proceeds" there while the entrustment said "warrants". This
-- builder cannot.
--
-- ONE PHRASE PER LIMB. The recital is built limb by limb, each limb carrying
-- its own adverb, and the phrases joined with ", and did ". That is not a
-- presentational choice: it is what keeps "dishonestly" off the third limb and
-- "intentionally" off the first two. See `the limbs relied on` below.
GIVEN f IS A `CBT Facts`
GIVETH A STRING
`the manner of the breach of trust` f MEANS
    CONCAT "being entrusted", (`by whom entrusted` f), " with ", (`what the accused was entrusted with` f),
           ", namely, ", f's property, " ", f's `purpose of the entrustment`,
           ", did ", (`the limbs relied on` f)

GIVEN f IS A `CBT Facts`
GIVETH A STRING
`by whom entrusted` f MEANS
    IF   f's `entrusted by` EQUALS ""
    THEN ""
    ELSE CONCAT " by ", f's `entrusted by`

GIVEN f IS A `CBT Facts`
GIVETH A STRING
`what the accused was entrusted with` f MEANS
    `joined with` " and " (concat (LIST
        (`included if` (f's `entrusted with property`) "property"),
        (`included if` (f's `entrusted with dominion over property`) "dominion over property")))

-- "dishonestly" governs the first two limbs; "intentionally" the third, and
-- the section keeps them on separate rails — Woon, Essential Criminal Law ch 8
-- p 192, draws "Dishonesty" as the head of the upper branch and "Intentionally
-- suffers another person to" as the head of the lower one, with nothing in the
-- figure letting one adverb reach the other branch's verbs.
--
-- This used to be built as two independent lists — every adverb that held,
-- then every verb that held — concatenated as "did <adverbs> <verbs>". That
-- flattens the two rails into one. A record with the first and third limbs both
-- TRUE recited "did dishonestly and intentionally misappropriate, and suffer
-- another person to misappropriate the said furniture", which alleges an
-- INTENTIONAL misappropriation and a DISHONEST suffering — two manners the
-- section does not state, on a charge whose manner CPC s 125 requires to be
-- stated. So each limb now carries its own adverb and they are joined.
GIVEN f IS A `CBT Facts`
GIVETH A STRING
`the limbs relied on` f MEANS
    `joined with` ", and did " (concat (LIST
        (`included if` (`the first or second limb is made out` f) (`what the accused himself did` f)),
        (`included if` (f's `intentionally suffers any other person to do so`) (`what the accused suffered another to do` f))))

-- The first two limbs, as the ladder tests them, named once because both the
-- recital and the refusal ask the same question. This is the same expression
-- the ladder builds out of inert prose; it is stated again here rather than
-- called, because a call would draw the first two limbs as one box and the
-- ladder exists to be clicked leaf by leaf.
GIVEN f IS A `CBT Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the first or second limb is made out` f MEANS
        f's dishonestly
    AND (    (    f's `misappropriates that property`
             OR   f's `converts that property to his own use` )
         OR  (    (    f's `uses that property`
                  OR   f's `disposes of that property` )
             AND  (    f's `any direction of law prescribing the mode in which such trust is to be discharged`
                  OR   f's `any legal contract, express or implied, which he has made touching the discharge of such trust` ) ) )

-- The accused's own dealing with the property: "dishonestly", then the verbs
-- that hold, then the property, then the violation clause the second limb
-- needs. The violation clause rides here because "in violation of" qualifies
-- the use-or-disposal limb and nothing else.
GIVEN f IS A `CBT Facts`
GIVETH A STRING
`what the accused himself did` f MEANS
    CONCAT "dishonestly ", (`what the accused did with it` f),
           " the said ", (`the property, in short` f), (`in violation of what` f)

-- THE ANAPHOR. The third limb is "intentionally suffers any other person to do
-- so", and "so" reaches back over everything before it: "dishonestly", the four
-- acts, "that property", and — for the use-or-disposal alternative — "in
-- violation of" as well. `CBT Facts` records THAT the accused suffered another
-- person to do so; it does not record WHICH of the four acts the other person
-- chose, and no field here can be made to say. So the recital carries the
-- anaphor rather than picking one of the four.
--
-- It used to pick, and it always picked the same one: the third limb mapped to
-- the fixed phrase "suffer another person to misappropriate", so a record on
-- which the other person had used or disposed of the property still recited a
-- misappropriation. That states a manner that did not happen, which is a defect
-- of form of the same species as the one this module exists to foreclose.
--
-- Carrying the other person's act instead would mean new fields on `CBT Facts`
-- — his dishonesty, which of the four acts he did, and the violation where he
-- used or disposed — and the ladder would have to test them. That is a larger
-- change than this one, and it is not made here: the anaphor is recited
-- faithfully instead, which is what the statute itself does.
GIVEN f IS A `CBT Facts`
GIVETH A STRING
`what the accused suffered another to do` f MEANS
    CONCAT "intentionally suffer another person so to do in respect of the said ", (`the property, in short` f)

GIVEN f IS A `CBT Facts`
GIVETH A STRING
`what the accused did with it` f MEANS
    `joined with` ", and " (concat (LIST
        (`included if` (f's `misappropriates that property`) "misappropriate"),
        (`included if` (f's `converts that property to his own use`) "convert to your own use"),
        (`included if` (f's `uses that property`) "use"),
        (`included if` (f's `disposes of that property`) "dispose of")))

-- The back-reference is a short form of the ONE property, never a second one.
-- Blank means "repeat it in full", which is always safe.
GIVEN f IS A `CBT Facts`
GIVETH A STRING
`the property, in short` f MEANS
    IF   f's `property, in short` EQUALS ""
    THEN f's property
    ELSE f's `property, in short`

GIVEN f IS A `CBT Facts`
GIVETH A STRING
`in violation of what` f MEANS
    IF   f's `any direction of law prescribing the mode in which such trust is to be discharged`
      OR f's `any legal contract, express or implied, which he has made touching the discharge of such trust`
    THEN " in violation of " APPEND (`joined with` ", or of " (concat (LIST
             (`included if` (f's `any direction of law prescribing the mode in which such trust is to be discharged`) "a direction of law prescribing the mode in which the said trust was to be discharged"),
             (`included if` (f's `any legal contract, express or implied, which he has made touching the discharge of such trust`) "a legal contract, express or implied, which you had made touching the discharge of the said trust"))))
    ELSE ""

-- The elements, named for the refusal. The second entry is the one the reported
-- charge failed, and it says out loud what "that property" refers back to.
GIVEN f IS A `CBT Facts`
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`elements of criminal breach of trust not made out` f MEANS
    concat (LIST
        (`missing unless` (f's `entrusted with property` OR f's `entrusted with dominion over property`) "being in any manner entrusted with property, or with any dominion over property"),
        (`missing unless` (f's dishonestly OR f's `intentionally suffers any other person to do so`) "dishonestly — which governs misappropriation, conversion, use and disposal — or intentionally, which governs suffering any other person to do so"),
        (`missing unless` ((`the first or second limb is made out` f) OR f's `intentionally suffers any other person to do so`) "dishonestly misappropriates or converts to his own use THAT property, dishonestly uses or disposes of THAT property in violation of a direction of law or of a legal contract, or intentionally suffers any other person to do so — where “that property” is the property entrusted and nothing else"),
        (`missing unless` (`commits criminal breach of trust` f) "the elements of section 405 taken together"))

@export Frame a charge under section 406 of the Penal Code 1871 (criminal breach of trust), or say why none can be framed
GIVEN f IS A `CBT Facts` @desc The entrustment, what became of the property, and the particulars of the charge
GIVETH A Charge
`charge under s 406` f MEANS
    `frame the charge` (f's particulars) "406" "criminal breach of trust"
        "imprisonment for a term which may extend to 7 years, or with fine, or with both"
        (`offence under s 406` f)
        (`the manner of the breach of trust` f)
        (`elements of criminal breach of trust not made out` f)

§§ `Carl Elias Moses v Public Prosecutor [1995] 3 SLR 748, as quoted in Viswanathan Ramachandran v Public Prosecutor [2003] SGHC 183`

-- The charge, as quoted in the 2003 judgment:
--
--   You, Carl Elias s/o Jack Moses Elias, NRIC No 1475884/D, are charged that
--   you, on or about 23 October 1989, in Singapore, being entrusted with
--   property, namely, 2,000 Overseas Union Bank Warrant 1994 for the purpose of
--   delivering these warrants on behalf of Trans-Pacific Credit Pte Ltd (`the
--   company`) to DBS Securities Pte Ltd pursuant to a sale (per contract number
--   414270/501) of the said warrants, and thereafter to pay the proceeds of the
--   said sale to the company, did dishonestly misappropriate the said proceeds,
--   and you have thereby committed an offence punishable under s 406 of the
--   Penal Code (Cap 224, 1985 Rev Ed).
--
-- He was entrusted with the warrants and he delivered the warrants, exactly as
-- directed. What he kept was the money DBS paid for them. So the honest answer
-- to `misappropriates that property` — asked of the property entrusted — is
-- FALSE, and no charge can be framed on this record.
`Carl Elias Moses, as charged` MEANS `CBT Facts` WITH
    particulars           IS Particulars WITH
                               accused            IS "Carl Elias Moses"
                               date               IS "23 October 1989"
                               time               IS ""
                               place              IS "the premises of Trans-Pacific Credit Pte Ltd"
                               `co-accused`       IS ""
                               `common intention` IS FALSE
    `entrusted by`        IS ""
    property              IS "2,000 Overseas Union Bank Warrant 1994"
    `property, in short`  IS "warrants"
    `purpose of the entrustment`
                          IS "for the purpose of delivering these warrants on behalf of Trans-Pacific Credit Pte Ltd (“the company”) to DBS Securities Pte Ltd pursuant to a sale (per contract number 414270/501) of the said warrants, and thereafter to pay the proceeds of the said sale to the company"
    `entrusted with property` IS TRUE
    `entrusted with dominion over property` IS FALSE
    dishonestly           IS TRUE
    `misappropriates that property` IS FALSE
    `converts that property to his own use` IS FALSE
    `uses that property`  IS FALSE
    `disposes of that property` IS FALSE
    `any direction of law prescribing the mode in which such trust is to be discharged` IS FALSE
    `any legal contract, express or implied, which he has made touching the discharge of such trust` IS FALSE
    `intentionally suffers any other person to do so` IS FALSE

-- The nearest thing to the flawed charge that this file CAN state. Answer the
-- misappropriation question TRUE and a charge is framed — but read its body: it
-- alleges misappropriation of the WARRANTS, which is a different allegation
-- from the one that was laid, and a false one on these facts. The words "the
-- said proceeds" cannot appear, because the property entrusted was not the
-- proceeds.
`Carl Elias Moses, with the warrants misappropriated` MEANS `CBT Facts` WITH
    particulars           IS `Carl Elias Moses, as charged`'s particulars
    `entrusted by`        IS `Carl Elias Moses, as charged`'s `entrusted by`
    property              IS `Carl Elias Moses, as charged`'s property
    `property, in short`  IS `Carl Elias Moses, as charged`'s `property, in short`
    `purpose of the entrustment`
                          IS `Carl Elias Moses, as charged`'s `purpose of the entrustment`
    `entrusted with property` IS TRUE
    `entrusted with dominion over property` IS FALSE
    dishonestly           IS TRUE
    `misappropriates that property` IS TRUE
    `converts that property to his own use` IS FALSE
    `uses that property`  IS FALSE
    `disposes of that property` IS FALSE
    `any direction of law prescribing the mode in which such trust is to be discharged` IS FALSE
    `any legal contract, express or implied, which he has made touching the discharge of such trust` IS FALSE
    `intentionally suffers any other person to do so` IS FALSE

-- The charge the court said should have been laid: move the ENTRUSTMENT onto
-- the proceeds, which is what the amendment allowed in Viswanathan Ramachandran
-- did on its own facts. The property is now one thing throughout, and the same
-- field supplies both clauses.
`Carl Elias Moses, the charge amended` MEANS `CBT Facts` WITH
    particulars           IS `Carl Elias Moses, as charged`'s particulars
    `entrusted by`        IS "Trans-Pacific Credit Pte Ltd (“the company”)"
    property              IS "the proceeds of the sale of 2,000 Overseas Union Bank Warrant 1994"
    `property, in short`  IS "proceeds"
    `purpose of the entrustment`
                          IS "received by you from DBS Securities Pte Ltd pursuant to a sale (per contract number 414270/501) of the said warrants, for the purpose of paying the same to the company"
    `entrusted with property` IS TRUE
    `entrusted with dominion over property` IS FALSE
    dishonestly           IS TRUE
    `misappropriates that property` IS TRUE
    `converts that property to his own use` IS FALSE
    `uses that property`  IS FALSE
    `disposes of that property` IS FALSE
    `any direction of law prescribing the mode in which such trust is to be discharged` IS FALSE
    `any legal contract, express or implied, which he has made touching the discharge of such trust` IS FALSE
    `intentionally suffers any other person to do so` IS FALSE

§§ `Illustrations (c) and (d) to section 405`

-- (c) A, residing in Singapore, is agent for Z, residing in Penang. There is an
--     express or implied contract between A and Z that all sums remitted by Z
--     to A shall be invested by A according to Z's direction. Z remits $5,000
--     to A, with directions to A to invest the same in Government securities. A
--     dishonestly disobeys the direction, and employs the money in his own
--     business. A has committed criminal breach of trust.
--
-- The illustration's own emphasis is on disobeying a direction given under a
-- contract, so this is the SECOND limb: a dishonest use of the property in
-- violation of a legal contract touching the discharge of the trust. Z's
-- direction is not a "direction of law", which is illustration (a)'s limb.
`the Penang agent, Illustration (c)` MEANS `CBT Facts` WITH
    particulars           IS Particulars WITH
                               accused            IS "A"
                               date               IS "a date unknown"
                               time               IS ""
                               place              IS "A's place of business"
                               `co-accused`       IS ""
                               `common intention` IS FALSE
    `entrusted by`        IS "one Z of Penang"
    property              IS "$5,000 remitted to you by the said Z"
    `property, in short`  IS "moneys"
    `purpose of the entrustment`
                          IS "with directions to you to invest the same in Government securities, pursuant to an express or implied contract between you and the said Z that all sums remitted by the said Z to you shall be invested by you according to his direction"
    `entrusted with property` IS TRUE
    `entrusted with dominion over property` IS FALSE
    dishonestly           IS TRUE
    `misappropriates that property` IS FALSE
    `converts that property to his own use` IS FALSE
    `uses that property`  IS TRUE
    `disposes of that property` IS FALSE
    `any direction of law prescribing the mode in which such trust is to be discharged` IS FALSE
    `any legal contract, express or implied, which he has made touching the discharge of such trust` IS TRUE
    `intentionally suffers any other person to do so` IS FALSE

-- (d) the same agent, acting in good faith. The illustration says in terms that
--     he "has not committed criminal breach of trust", so this is the statute's
--     own negative oracle for the `dishonestly` leaf.
`the Penang agent in good faith, Illustration (d)` MEANS `CBT Facts` WITH
    particulars           IS `the Penang agent, Illustration (c)`'s particulars
    `entrusted by`        IS `the Penang agent, Illustration (c)`'s `entrusted by`
    property              IS `the Penang agent, Illustration (c)`'s property
    `property, in short`  IS `the Penang agent, Illustration (c)`'s `property, in short`
    `purpose of the entrustment`
                          IS `the Penang agent, Illustration (c)`'s `purpose of the entrustment`
    `entrusted with property` IS TRUE
    `entrusted with dominion over property` IS FALSE
    dishonestly           IS FALSE
    `misappropriates that property` IS FALSE
    `converts that property to his own use` IS FALSE
    `uses that property`  IS TRUE
    `disposes of that property` IS FALSE
    `any direction of law prescribing the mode in which such trust is to be discharged` IS FALSE
    `any legal contract, express or implied, which he has made touching the discharge of such trust` IS TRUE
    `intentionally suffers any other person to do so` IS FALSE

§§ `The third limb, on facts constructed from Illustration (b)`

-- NO illustration to section 405 turns on the third limb, and no charge on this
-- row's bench was laid under it, so these two records are CONSTRUCTED, not
-- reported. They take Illustration (b)'s entrustment — the warehouse operator
-- and Z's furniture — and put the dealing in someone else's hands. They exist
-- because the recital of the third limb had no fixture at all, and two defects
-- of form lived there undisturbed for want of one.
--
-- (b) A is a warehouse owner or warehouse operator. Z, going on a journey,
--     entrusts his furniture to A, under a contract that it shall be returned
--     on payment of a stipulated sum for warehouse room. A dishonestly sells
--     the goods. A has committed breach of trust.
--
-- The accused here does nothing to the furniture himself. He intentionally
-- suffers another person to do so, and "so" carries that other person's
-- dishonesty with it (s 405; the leaf's @desc says so). `dishonestly` on this
-- record is the ACCUSED's own dishonesty in his own dealing, and he had none,
-- because he had no dealing — which is exactly why the section gives the third
-- limb its own adverb.
`the warehouse operator who suffered another, constructed` MEANS `CBT Facts` WITH
    particulars           IS Particulars WITH
                               accused            IS "A"
                               date               IS "a date unknown"
                               time               IS ""
                               place              IS "your warehouse"
                               `co-accused`       IS ""
                               `common intention` IS FALSE
    `entrusted by`        IS "one Z"
    property              IS "the furniture of the said Z"
    `property, in short`  IS "furniture"
    `purpose of the entrustment`
                          IS "under a contract that the same shall be returned to the said Z on payment of a stipulated sum for warehouse room"
    `entrusted with property` IS TRUE
    `entrusted with dominion over property` IS FALSE
    dishonestly           IS FALSE
    `misappropriates that property` IS FALSE
    `converts that property to his own use` IS FALSE
    `uses that property`  IS FALSE
    `disposes of that property` IS FALSE
    `any direction of law prescribing the mode in which such trust is to be discharged` IS FALSE
    `any legal contract, express or implied, which he has made touching the discharge of such trust` IS FALSE
    `intentionally suffers any other person to do so` IS TRUE

-- The same operator, who also helped himself to part of the furniture. Both
-- the FIRST and the THIRD limb are made out, which is the only arrangement of
-- facts on which the two adverbs can collide.
`the warehouse operator who also misappropriated, constructed` MEANS `CBT Facts` WITH
    particulars           IS `the warehouse operator who suffered another, constructed`'s particulars
    `entrusted by`        IS `the warehouse operator who suffered another, constructed`'s `entrusted by`
    property              IS `the warehouse operator who suffered another, constructed`'s property
    `property, in short`  IS `the warehouse operator who suffered another, constructed`'s `property, in short`
    `purpose of the entrustment`
                          IS `the warehouse operator who suffered another, constructed`'s `purpose of the entrustment`
    `entrusted with property` IS TRUE
    `entrusted with dominion over property` IS FALSE
    dishonestly           IS TRUE
    `misappropriates that property` IS TRUE
    `converts that property to his own use` IS FALSE
    `uses that property`  IS FALSE
    `disposes of that property` IS FALSE
    `any direction of law prescribing the mode in which such trust is to be discharged` IS FALSE
    `any legal contract, express or implied, which he has made touching the discharge of such trust` IS FALSE
    `intentionally suffers any other person to do so` IS TRUE

§§ `Directives`

#EVAL `commits criminal breach of trust` `Carl Elias Moses, as charged`
#EVAL (`charge under s 406` `Carl Elias Moses, as charged`)'s refusal
#EVAL (`charge under s 406` `Carl Elias Moses, with the warrants misappropriated`)'s text
#EVAL (`charge under s 406` `Carl Elias Moses, the charge amended`)'s text
#EVAL (`charge under s 406` `the Penang agent, Illustration (c)`)'s text
#EVAL (`charge under s 406` `the Penang agent in good faith, Illustration (d)`)'s refusal

-- The charge AS LAID cannot be framed. Asked about the property entrusted —
-- the warrants, which he delivered exactly as directed — the misappropriation
-- element answers FALSE, so no text is written and the refusal names the limb
-- together with what “that property” refers back to.
#ASSERT NOT (`commits criminal breach of trust` `Carl Elias Moses, as charged`)
#ASSERT NOT ((`charge under s 406` `Carl Elias Moses, as charged`)'s `made out`)
#ASSERT (`charge under s 406` `Carl Elias Moses, as charged`)'s text EQUALS ""
#ASSERT (`charge under s 406` `Carl Elias Moses, as charged`)'s refusal EQUALS "A charge under section 406 would assert that the facts fulfil every legal condition of criminal breach of trust (CPC s 123(5)). On the facts given they do not: dishonestly misappropriates or converts to his own use THAT property, dishonestly uses or disposes of THAT property in violation of a direction of law or of a legal contract, or intentionally suffers any other person to do so — where “that property” is the property entrusted and nothing else; the elements of section 405 taken together."

-- THE DEMONSTRATION. Answer the misappropriation question TRUE and a charge
-- IS framed — but its body says “the said warrants”, not “the said proceeds”.
-- That is a different allegation from the one the Prosecution laid, and the
-- charge the Prosecution laid is not among the strings this module can emit.
#ASSERT (`charge under s 406` `Carl Elias Moses, with the warrants misappropriated`)'s `made out`
#ASSERT (`charge under s 406` `Carl Elias Moses, with the warrants misappropriated`)'s text EQUALS "You, Carl Elias Moses, are charged that you, on or about 23 October 1989, at the premises of Trans-Pacific Credit Pte Ltd, Singapore, being entrusted with property, namely, 2,000 Overseas Union Bank Warrant 1994 for the purpose of delivering these warrants on behalf of Trans-Pacific Credit Pte Ltd (“the company”) to DBS Securities Pte Ltd pursuant to a sale (per contract number 414270/501) of the said warrants, and thereafter to pay the proceeds of the said sale to the company, did dishonestly misappropriate the said warrants, and you have thereby committed an offence punishable under section 406 of the Penal Code 1871."

-- The charge the court said should have been laid: the entrustment moved onto
-- the proceeds, so one property serves both clauses.
#ASSERT `commits criminal breach of trust` `Carl Elias Moses, the charge amended`
#ASSERT `offence under s 406` `Carl Elias Moses, the charge amended`
#ASSERT (`charge under s 406` `Carl Elias Moses, the charge amended`)'s `made out`
#ASSERT (`charge under s 406` `Carl Elias Moses, the charge amended`)'s text EQUALS "You, Carl Elias Moses, are charged that you, on or about 23 October 1989, at the premises of Trans-Pacific Credit Pte Ltd, Singapore, being entrusted by Trans-Pacific Credit Pte Ltd (“the company”) with property, namely, the proceeds of the sale of 2,000 Overseas Union Bank Warrant 1994 received by you from DBS Securities Pte Ltd pursuant to a sale (per contract number 414270/501) of the said warrants, for the purpose of paying the same to the company, did dishonestly misappropriate the said proceeds, and you have thereby committed an offence punishable under section 406 of the Penal Code 1871."
#ASSERT (`charge under s 406` `Carl Elias Moses, the charge amended`)'s punishment EQUALS "imprisonment for a term which may extend to 7 years, or with fine, or with both"

-- Illustration (c): the section's own positive case, on the second limb.
#ASSERT `commits criminal breach of trust` `the Penang agent, Illustration (c)`
#ASSERT (`charge under s 406` `the Penang agent, Illustration (c)`)'s `made out`
#ASSERT (`charge under s 406` `the Penang agent, Illustration (c)`)'s text EQUALS "You, A, are charged that you, on or about a date unknown, at A's place of business, Singapore, being entrusted by one Z of Penang with property, namely, $5,000 remitted to you by the said Z with directions to you to invest the same in Government securities, pursuant to an express or implied contract between you and the said Z that all sums remitted by the said Z to you shall be invested by you according to his direction, did dishonestly use the said moneys in violation of a legal contract, express or implied, which you had made touching the discharge of the said trust, and you have thereby committed an offence punishable under section 406 of the Penal Code 1871."

-- Illustration (d): the same agent in good faith. The illustration says in
-- terms that he has NOT committed criminal breach of trust, so this is the
-- statute's own negative oracle for the `dishonestly` leaf.
#ASSERT NOT (`commits criminal breach of trust` `the Penang agent in good faith, Illustration (d)`)
#ASSERT NOT ((`charge under s 406` `the Penang agent in good faith, Illustration (d)`)'s `made out`)
#ASSERT (`charge under s 406` `the Penang agent in good faith, Illustration (d)`)'s refusal EQUALS "A charge under section 406 would assert that the facts fulfil every legal condition of criminal breach of trust (CPC s 123(5)). On the facts given they do not: dishonestly — which governs misappropriation, conversion, use and disposal — or intentionally, which governs suffering any other person to do so; dishonestly misappropriates or converts to his own use THAT property, dishonestly uses or disposes of THAT property in violation of a direction of law or of a legal contract, or intentionally suffers any other person to do so — where “that property” is the property entrusted and nothing else; the elements of section 405 taken together."

-- THE THIRD LIMB, RECITED. Neither of the next two assertions could be written
-- before 2026-09-21, because no fixture reached the third limb; both would have
-- FAILED against the recital as it then stood.
--
-- The ladder is untouched by this change, and these two lines say so: both
-- records made out section 405 before and make it out now.
#ASSERT `commits criminal breach of trust` `the warehouse operator who suffered another, constructed`
#ASSERT `commits criminal breach of trust` `the warehouse operator who also misappropriated, constructed`

-- The anaphor. The recital used to read "did intentionally suffer another
-- person to MISAPPROPRIATE the said furniture" on this record — a manner
-- nobody alleged, since the record does not say which of the four acts the
-- other person did. It now recites the section's own "so".
#ASSERT (`charge under s 406` `the warehouse operator who suffered another, constructed`)'s `made out`
#ASSERT (`charge under s 406` `the warehouse operator who suffered another, constructed`)'s text EQUALS "You, A, are charged that you, on or about a date unknown, at your warehouse, Singapore, being entrusted by one Z with property, namely, the furniture of the said Z under a contract that the same shall be returned to the said Z on payment of a stipulated sum for warehouse room, did intentionally suffer another person so to do in respect of the said furniture, and you have thereby committed an offence punishable under section 406 of the Penal Code 1871."

-- The adverbs. With the first and third limbs both made out the recital used to
-- read "did dishonestly and intentionally misappropriate, and suffer another
-- person to misappropriate the said furniture", putting each adverb in front of
-- the other limb's verb. Each limb now carries its own.
#ASSERT `the limbs relied on` `the warehouse operator who also misappropriated, constructed` EQUALS "dishonestly misappropriate the said furniture, and did intentionally suffer another person so to do in respect of the said furniture"
#ASSERT (`charge under s 406` `the warehouse operator who also misappropriated, constructed`)'s text EQUALS "You, A, are charged that you, on or about a date unknown, at your warehouse, Singapore, being entrusted by one Z with property, namely, the furniture of the said Z under a contract that the same shall be returned to the said Z on payment of a stipulated sum for warehouse room, did dishonestly misappropriate the said furniture, and did intentionally suffer another person so to do in respect of the said furniture, and you have thereby committed an offence punishable under section 406 of the Penal Code 1871."
